    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "List them",
        "body": "Let the sides be $a < b < c$ (scalene) with $a + b > c$ and $a + b + c \\le 10$.\n\n- $a = 1$: we would need $c < 1 + b$, i.e. $c \\le b$ — impossible since $c > b$.\n- $a = 2$: $c < b + 2$ and $c > b$ force $c = b + 1$; perimeter $2b + 3 \\le 10$ gives $b \\le 3$, so $b = 3$, $c = 4$: triangle $(2, 3, 4)$.\n- $a = 3$: $b \\ge 4$, $c \\ge 5$, perimeter $\\ge 12$ — too big.\n\nOnly $(2, 3, 4)$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 1**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Check the perimeters",
        "body": "The smallest scalene integer triangles are $(2,3,4)$ with perimeter 9, then $(2,4,5)$ and $(3,4,5)$ with perimeters 11 and 12. Triples like $(1,2,3)$, $(2,3,5)$, $(1,3,4)$ are degenerate or impossible. So there is exactly one with perimeter below 11.\n\n**Answer: (B)**"
      }
    ],
