    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Several non-overlapping isosceles triangles have vertex O in common. Every triangle shares an edge with each immediate neighbour. The smallest angle of a triangle at O has size m°, where m is a positive integer. The other triangles have angles at O of size 2m°, 3m°, 4m°, and so on. The diagram shows an arrangement of five such triangles. What is the smallest value of m, for which such a set of triangles exists?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2013-g0910/q23.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "6"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "8"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "The angles at O fill a full turn",
        "body": "As in the diagram, the triangles go all the way round $O$, so with $n$ triangles\n$$m + 2m + \\dots + nm = m\\cdot\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = 360.$$\nSo $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ must be a divisor of 360, and $m = 360 \\div \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$.\n\n- $m = 1$ needs $n(n+1) = 720$: but $26\\cdot 27 = 702$, $27 \\cdot 28 = 756$ — impossible.\n- $m = 2$ needs $n(n+1) = 360$: $18 \\cdot 19 = 342$, $19 \\cdot 20 = 380$ — impossible.\n- $m = 3$ needs $n(n+1) = 240 = 15 \\cdot 16$ ✓, so $n = 15$.\n\nFor $m = 3$ take 15 isosceles triangles with apex $O$, all with legs of the same length and apex angles $3^\\circ, 6^\\circ, \\dots, 45^\\circ$ (sum $3 \\cdot 120 = 360^\\circ$). Neighbours share a full leg, so this arrangement works.\n\n**Answer: (C) 3**"
      }
    ],
